-- std.scalar — methods on integer and bool values.
-- Bit operations use the runtime width: 64 bits for i64, u64, isize and usize,
-- and 128 bits for i128 and u128. A negative rotate distance is a runtime error.
-- Distances at or above the width wrap. ilog2 and ilog10 of zero or a negative
-- value are null. abs_diff returns the unsigned width of its inputs.
-- wrapping_* keeps the low bits. saturating_* stops at the numeric bounds.
-- checked_* returns null on overflow. checked_div and checked_rem also return
-- null when the divisor is zero. Both operands must share a runtime width.
-- div_euclid and rem_euclid reject a zero divisor. The signed minimum divided
-- or remaindered by -1 is NumericOverflow. A negative shift or exponent is a
-- runtime error. checked_shl and checked_shr return null when the distance is
-- at least the width. checked_pow returns null on overflow. unsigned_abs is
-- only defined for signed values. wrapping_div and wrapping_rem also reject a
-- zero divisor, but the signed minimum divided by -1 wraps. saturating_abs,
-- checked_abs, wrapping_abs, and signum are signed only. checked_ilog returns
-- null for a non-positive value; a base below 2 is a runtime error.
-- cast_unsigned and cast_signed keep the bits and change only the sign.
-- checked_div_euclid and checked_rem_euclid return null for a zero divisor and
-- for the signed minimum divided by -1. widening_mul is the full 64-bit product
-- at 128 bits. next_power_of_two is unsigned; overflow is an error, and the
-- checked form returns null. Zero's next power is 1. A divisor of zero makes
-- is_multiple_of false.
-- is_power_of_two is false for zero and for every negative value.
-- strict_* reports NumericOverflow instead of wrapping or returning null.
-- A zero divisor is still an error. A negative shift or exponent is an error.
-- saturating_div and saturating_div_euclid stop at the signed maximum when
-- the minimum is divided by -1.
-- div_ceil rounds toward positive infinity. A zero divisor is an error, and
-- overflow is NumericOverflow. checked_div_ceil returns null for both.
-- next_multiple_of moves toward the sign of the factor. A zero factor is an
-- error. to_be_bytes and to_le_bytes list the raw bytes.
-- saturating_add_signed, wrapping_add_signed, and strict_add_signed take an
-- unsigned receiver and a same-width signed operand. Saturating stops at 0
-- and the unsigned maximum. Wrapping keeps the low bits. Strict reports
-- NumericOverflow. The sub forms use the same rules.
-- unbounded_shl and unbounded_shr do not wrap the distance. A distance at
-- least the width yields 0, except a negative value shifted right yields -1.
-- A negative distance is an error.
-- i64_min, i64_max, u64_max, i128_min, i128_max, and u128_max are the bounds.
-- isize_min, isize_max, and usize_max reuse the 64-bit bounds.
-- highest_one and lowest_one are bit indexes. Zero is null. isolate_* keeps
-- only that bit, and zero stays zero. bit_width counts the two's-complement
-- bits, so every negative value uses the full width and zero uses none.
-- i64_bits is 64, i128_bits and u128_bits are 128, and isize_bits and
-- usize_bits are 64.
-- isqrt rounds down. A negative value is an error, and checked_isqrt returns
-- null. strict_abs is signed only; the minimum is NumericOverflow.
-- wrapping_div_euclid rounds toward negative infinity and wraps the signed
-- minimum divided by -1. wrapping_rem_euclid of that pair is 0.
-- checked_signed_diff subtracts unsigned values into the signed width.
-- checked_div_exact returns null for a zero divisor, overflow, or a remainder.
-- strict_div_exact reports a remainder as InvalidArgument("division is not exact").
-- Exact shifts are reversible. A distance at least the width is null, or
-- NumericOverflow for the strict form. Lost bits are null, or
-- InvalidArgument("shift is not exact").

